WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.631
Three simple code lookups.

2
00:00:02.831 --> 00:00:06.239
Four turns. 294k tokens.

3
00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:10.772
Every turn, an AI coding agent
reads its whole conversation again.

4
00:00:11.422 --> 00:00:14.083
Now the same lookups, with PeterGanuRusty.

5
00:00:14.303 --> 00:00:15.330
One question.

6
00:00:15.550 --> 00:00:20.278
One answer. The stream
shortens to 134k tokens.

7
00:00:20.498 --> 00:00:23.710
54% fewer than no index, in 2 turns.

8
00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:27.370
That is one comparison:
light class, first question set.

9
00:00:28.104 --> 00:00:29.794
It gives an agent four things.

10
00:00:30.094 --> 00:00:33.546
Where a function is defined,
and what calls it, in one lookup.

11
00:00:33.846 --> 00:00:37.061
Search by meaning: describe
a job, get the function.

12
00:00:37.361 --> 00:00:39.505
What a change reaches, before the edit.

13
00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:42.234
And findings, remembered between sessions.

14
00:00:42.943 --> 00:00:49.578
To measure it, we ran 884 agents on
one Python repository of 86 files.

15
00:00:49.878 --> 00:00:52.616
Three questions, graded
against the syntax tree.

16
00:00:52.916 --> 00:00:55.422
Three model classes: light, mid and top.

17
00:00:55.722 --> 00:01:00.821
14 ways to find code, fewest tokens
first. The most reliable is marked.

18
00:01:01.121 --> 00:01:07.682
Against no index: 28% to 54% fewer
tokens, across 4 comparisons.

19
00:01:07.982 --> 00:01:12.420
The most reliable was fully correct
in every run, on both question sets.

20
00:01:13.699 --> 00:01:17.929
The limits are real: one repository,
a few runs for each setup.

21
00:01:18.379 --> 00:01:20.768
The study measures the
first two things only.

22
00:01:21.218 --> 00:01:23.644
PeterGanuRusty. Private preview.
